  "query_preview": "..Our period of somewhat decreased monsoonal activity\nwill continue today and tomorrow as strong high pressure is \nparked over Arizona. While the magnitude of the high is weakening \nslightly, northern Arizona will remain in a relatively drier and \ncapped environment. We are not totally dried out, so expect \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms each \nafternoon/evening along the higher terrain Flagstaff and eastward,\nwith the best coverage in the White Mountains.\n\nThe primary hazard today and tomorrow remains to be the heat. Some \nlocations saw their warmest temperatures of the year so far on \nSaturday, and a few daily records were broken. With the high \nslightly weakening over the next few days, daytime temperatures will \nbe just a few degrees cooler. However, temperatures will remain hot \nenough for the Extreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory to remain in \neffect until at least Monday evening. Be sure to drink plenty of \nfluids and avoid prolonged outdoor activity if you can!\n\nThe high continues to weaken and elongate on Tuesday. Below normal \nmonsoonal moisture is expected to linger, and the forecast calls for \nisolated to scattered storms constrained higher terrain of eastern \nArizona. The slight cooling trend also continues, and most heat \nheadlines will be over by Tuesday. Though temperatures at Phantom\nRanch look to stay high enough, and the Extreme Heat Warning for \nthe Grand Canyon below 4000 feet will continue through Tuesday \nevening. The high is forecast to shift during the middle to later \npart of this week, however, there is considerable uncertainty with\nthe exact placement. Clusters are split between a wetter scenario\nwhere the high is centered more to our north, or a drier scenario\nwhere the high is centered more to our northwest. The grand \nensemble consensus is for an increase in shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage starting Wednesday and continuing through the weekend, \nbut there is plenty of room for the exact details to change. Be \nsure to stay tuned for further updates!",
